---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
import { SEGMENTS } from '../../data/segments';
const path = '/for';
const title = 'Cart recovery by Shopify store type';
const desc = 'Choose a recovery workflow around the unresolved purchase decision, product constraints and message ownership.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="container py-16 md:py-24">
<h1 class="hero-title">Cart recovery by Shopify store type</h1>
<article class="prose-shopify mt-12 max-w-4xl">
<p>A store category helps identify what to investigate; it does not establish a universal send time, basket value or winning app. Start with the unresolved purchase question and the data your workflow can actually use. These guides describe decision criteria and proposed checks, rather than merchant test results.</p>
<h2>Choose the constraint that can invalidate the reminder</h2>
<p>For fashion, retain size and color and resolve fit. For jewelry, check personalization, proof and the gift date. Home goods add dimensions, configuration and delivery access. High-value purchases may require an owned consultation. Digital goods need a clear boundary between unpaid checkout and paid access support. Food and pet catalogs need purchase-type and subscription coordination; perishable food also needs delivery feasibility.</p>
<h2>Mixed catalogs need line-level decisions</h2>
<p>A basket containing a download and a physical item cannot inherit a single shipping promise. A subscription line and a one-time purchase need different terms. Identify which unavailable item should suppress a whole reminder and when it is appropriate to offer the remaining basket. Keep the shopper’s choice explicit instead of silently substituting products.</p>
<h2>Confirm eligibility before tuning timing</h2>
<p>Read <a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/abandoned-checkouts">Shopify’s checkout recovery documentation</a>. A cart event is not interchangeable with an eligible checkout. Assign one owner for each message and inspect queued sends when an order arrives. When changing providers, inventory old automations as well as the new workflow so the same checkout does not receive both.</p>
<h2>Build a practical selection brief</h2>
<p>Record the entry event, necessary product properties, purchase exit, channel permissions and support handoff. Ask each candidate to demonstrate your hardest basket, such as an engraved item, an unavailable size or a changed delivery cutoff. Save what happened and the unresolved limitation. A generic integration badge or a ranked shortlist cannot answer whether those properties survive a recovered checkout.</p>
<h2>Proposed measurement plan</h2>
<p>Choose a message change that addresses a specific barrier, such as sizing help or delivery clarification. Define the comparison, observation period and decision metric before activation. Estimate the sample required from your own baseline and the effect worth detecting; there is no universal minimum number of carts or holdout percentage. Allow time for cancellations and returns, especially for gifts and high-value purchases.</p>
<p>Report recovered orders separately from incremental lift. Deduct the full revenue discount, goods cost and relevant variable costs when comparing contribution. Keep assisted sales, subscription duplicates and unavailable-product failures visible. Segment results by the constraint being addressed so a blended recovery rate does not conceal a costly failure mode.</p>
<h2>Use the guides as working documents</h2>
<p>Revisit the relevant guide when adding custom options, changing a fulfillment promise or changing subscription providers. Keep an owner and a dated record of proposed checks and observed results. Some linked guides may still contain editorial issues; this hub does not certify their accuracy or establish app performance.</p>
</article>
<div class="mt-12 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
{SEGMENTS.map((s) => (
<a class="card recovery-card" href={`/for/${s.slug}`}><h2 class="text-xl font-black">{s.name}</h2><p class="mt-2 text-sm">Review recovery decisions for this catalog.</p></a>
))}
</div>
</section>
</BaseLayout>
